.TH KEYFS 4
.SH NAME
keyfs \- encrypted key storage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth/keyfs
[
.B -D
]
[
.BI -m " mountpoint"
] [
.BI -n " nvram"
] [
.I keyfile
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Keyfs
serves a two-level name space for storing authentication data, specifically
the status and secrets of each user to whom
.IR logind (8)
can issue a certificate.
The data is stored in
.I keyfile
(default:
.BR /keydb/keys ),
encrypted by a master key using AES
(see
.IR keyring-crypt (2)).
.I Keyfs
should be started only on the machine acting as authentication server (signer),
before a listener is started for
.IR signer (8).
Note that
.I signer
and
.I keyfs
must share the name space.
Furthermore, no other application except the console should see that name space.
.PP
.I Keyfs
prompts for the master key, reads and decrypts
.IR keyfile ,
and serves files representing the contents at
.I mountpoint
in the name space (default:
.BR /mnt/keys ).
.I Keyfs
does not make
.IR keyfile :
an administrator creates it, empty.
An empty file is a store not yet begun:
.I keyfs
says so, asks for the master key a second time to confirm it
(unless it comes from
.BR -n ),
and the file is written under that key at the first change.
A file that is not empty and does not open under the key given is refused,
and nothing is served.
.PP
Each
.I user
in
.I keyfile
is represented by a directory
.IB mountpoint / user.
Each such directory has the following files:
.TF status
.TP
.B log
A count of the number of failed authentications.
Writing
.B bad
to the file increments the count; writing
.B good
resets it to 0.
The count stops at 255.
When the count reaches some implementation-defined limit,
the account status is set to
.B disabled
(see the
.B status
file below).
.TP
.B expire
The time in seconds since the epoch when the account will expire,
or the text
.B never
if it has no expiration time.
The string
.B never
or a number can be written to the file to set a new expiry time;
a number greater than 2147483647 is refused.
.TP
.B secret
The secret (supposedly) known only to the user and the authentication service.
A secret is any sequence of bytes between 0 and 255 bytes long;
it is initially empty.
The length of the file returned by
.IR sys-stat (2)
is the length of the secret.
If the account has expired or is disabled, an attempt to read the file
will give an error.
.TP
.B status
The current status of the user's account, either
.B ok
or
.BR disabled .
Either string can be written to the file to change the state accordingly.
.PD
.PP
To add a new account, make a directory with that name in
.IR mountpoint .
It must not already exist.
To remove an account, remove the corresponding directory;
to rename an account, rename the directory.
A user's name is at most 255 bytes of
.SM UTF-8.
.PP
All changes made via file system operations in
.I mountpoint
result in appropriate changes to
.IR keyfile .
Each change writes the whole file anew as
.IB keyfile .new
and renames that to
.IR keyfile ,
so that a crash leaves either the old file or the new one, never a part of one.
Where the file server will not rename a file to a name in use, the old file is removed first;
if
.I keyfs
finds only
.IB keyfile .new
when it starts, it takes that, saying so, and renames it.
.PP
.I Keyfile
is made mode 600 whenever it is written.
If
.I keyfs
finds it open to users other than its owner when it starts
(it keeps the mode it was created with until its first change),
it sets the mode to 600 and says that it did:
whoever could read the file could have copied it,
and then the master key and the users' secrets should be changed.
.SS "File format"
.I Keyfile
is, in order: the eight bytes
.RB ` "keyfs 2\en" ';
a count of iterations, four bytes, most significant first;
a random salt of 16 bytes, made when the file is first written;
a random nonce of 12 bytes, new each time the file is written;
the entries encrypted by AES-256 in Galois/Counter Mode (SP 800-38D)
under that nonce, with the 40 bytes before them as associated data;
and the 16-byte tag.
The key is the first 32 bytes of PBKDF2 (RFC 8018) with HMAC-SHA-384
of the master key, the salt and the count of iterations (200000 for a new file).
What is encrypted is the number of entries, four bytes, most significant first,
then each entry:
a status byte (0 ok, 1 disabled),
the count of failed attempts (one byte),
the expiry time (four bytes, least significant first; 0 is never),
the length of the name and the name,
the length of the secret and the secret.
A changed, missing or added byte anywhere in the file makes
.I keyfs
refuse it.
An entry that is not well formed is refused too, with the entry's number.
.PP
Until this format the file was that of Plan 9's
.IR aescbc :
a 16-byte initialisation vector, then the entries and sixteen
.RB ` X 's
encrypted by AES-128 in CBC mode under the first 16 bytes of the SHA-1 of
.RB ` "aescbc file" '
and the master key.
It had no check of its integrity beyond those sixteen bytes,
and a copy of it tested a guess at the master key with one hash.
.I Keyfs
still reads a file in that format, and writes it in the new format at once,
as it starts (beside the old file and renamed over it, as every change is),
saying that it has; a copy of the file made before then is still in the old format.
(The file was once left until its next change, and a store nobody changed stayed
one SHA-1 from its master key.)
If the file cannot be rewritten (the store cannot be written),
.I keyfs
serves it as it is and prints a warning that it is in the old format at every start,
until it can.
Nothing writes the old format.
.PD
.PP
If the
.B -n
option is given, instead of prompting for the master key,
.I keyfs
will read it from the file
.IR nvram .
Obviously that file should be well-protected from ordinary observers.
The key is the file's bytes (at most 256) less one final newline,
and a carriage return before it, if there is one:
a file made by
.IR echo (1)
holds the key that is typed at the prompt.
(The newline was once part of the key, so the same password typed and kept in a file
were two keys.)
A store that was made under the key with its newline is opened by trying
that key second, and is then put under the key without it,
.I keyfs
saying so; if it cannot be rewritten it is served, with a warning at every start.
.PP
.I Keyfs
serves one user, the one that started it, which mounts it once:
an attach in another name is refused,
and so is a remove or a rename (wstat) by any other.
The files' modes say what each file is (a user's directory holds its four files and no others,
so it is not writable), not who may do what:
whoever reaches the mount point acts as that user, for every file.
The protection is the name space
.I keyfs
is mounted in, which a signer keeps to its own authentication services (see
.IR svc (8)).
.PP
.I Keyfs
does not start where it cannot read the time
.RB ( /dev/time ):
no account would expire.
.PP
The
.B -D
option enables tracing of the file service protocol, for debugging.
.SH FILES
.TF /keydb/keys.new
.TP
.B /keydb/keys
the default
.I keyfile
.TP
.B /keydb/keys.new
the file a change is written to before it is renamed
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auth/keyfs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR keys (6),
.IR changelogin (8),
.IR logind (8),
.IR signer (8)
